Download [the component](../FilterableList508.jsx) and [its pure predicate](../filter_508.mjs) into the same folder in a React application. The example IDs are numbers despite the text contract saying strings; React keys work for either stable unique type. The filter does not change items or their order.

The query is normalized once per predicate call for readability; for a larger dataset normalize once before calling `filter`. An empty trimmed query passes every item. Each select independently skips its check when set to `all`. Reset sets exactly the initial values and therefore restores the entire input list.

### Follow the examples and measure before optimizing

“garage” matches the names of items two and three. Adding active status removes item two. “door” matches descriptions of cameras, but combining it with sensor type leaves no result, so the explicit empty state appears. The pure function was checked against all four supplied examples, including Reset's initial filter values.

`useMemo` avoids repeating filtering when a parent rerenders with the same item-array identity and controls. It is an optimization, not a correctness requirement. Parents must replace arrays when data changes rather than mutating the same array in place. For a few thousand rows, linear filtering is usually a good starting point; memoization does not make a changed query sublinear or prevent rendering every visible row. If measured DOM rendering becomes slow, add virtualization separately. Labels, native controls, a polite result count and stable keys support accessible interaction without inventing a custom control behavior.

### Distinguish data work from DOM work

Virtualization renders only the visible rows. It does not make searching/sorting 100,000 records cheap, nor should sorting the current page be presented as sorting the whole dataset. Choose server-side filtering/sorting and pagination, then virtualize each fetched page. Assume fixed-height rows and an existing API that sorts the entire matching dataset before slicing a page.

![W510. Front-end Large Scale Data Rendering with React — architecture](../diagrams/W510_review_architecture.svg)

[Open the architecture diagram](../diagrams/W510_review_architecture.svg).

### React implementation

[Download the component](../LargeList510.jsx). Inject a stable `fetchPage` function returning `{items,total,version}`. It must respect the requested global sort, include a unique-ID tie-break for equal sort values and forward the AbortSignal to fetch.

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

// fetchPage({query,sort,direction,page,pageSize,signal}) -> {items,total,version}
export function LargeList({ fetchPage, dataVersion = 0 }) {
  const [text, setText] = useState("");
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [sort, setSort] = useState("name");
  const [direction, setDirection] = useState("asc");
  const [page, setPage] = useState(0);
  const [result, setResult] = useState({ items: [], total: 0 });
  const [status, setStatus] = useState("loading");
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);
  const generation = useRef(0);
  const viewport = useRef(null);
  const pageSize = 1000,
    rowHeight = 40,
    height = 400,
    overscan = 4;
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      setQuery(text.trim());
      setPage(0);
    }, 250);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [text]);
  useEffect(() => {
    const current = ++generation.current;
    const abort = new AbortController();
    setStatus("loading");
    setScrollTop(0);
    if (viewport.current) viewport.current.scrollTop = 0;
    fetchPage({ query, sort, direction, page, pageSize, signal: abort.signal })
      .then((data) => {
        if (current === generation.current && !abort.signal.aborted) {
          setResult(data);
          setStatus("ready");
        }
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        if (current === generation.current && !abort.signal.aborted) setStatus("error");
      });
    return () => abort.abort();
  }, [query, sort, direction, page, fetchPage, dataVersion]);
  const start = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - overscan);
  const end = Math.min(
    result.items.length,
    Math.ceil((scrollTop + height) / rowHeight) + overscan,
  );
  return (
    <section>
      <label>
        Search <input value={text} onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)} />
      </label>
      <label>
        Sort{" "}
        <select
          value={sort}
          onChange={(e) => {
            setSort(e.target.value);
            setPage(0);
          }}
        >
          <option value="name">Name</option>
          <option value="updatedAt">Updated</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <button
        onClick={() => {
          setDirection((d) => (d === "asc" ? "desc" : "asc"));
          setPage(0);
        }}
      >
        {direction === "asc" ? "Ascending" : "Descending"}
      </button>
      <button
        disabled={page === 0 || status === "loading"}
        onClick={() => setPage((p) => p - 1)}
      >
        Previous
      </button>
      <button
        disabled={(page + 1) * pageSize >= result.total || status === "loading"}
        onClick={() => setPage((p) => p + 1)}
      >
        Next
      </button>
      <p aria-live="polite">
        {status === "ready" ? `${result.total} results, page ${page + 1}` : status}
      </p>
      {status === "ready" && (
        <div
          ref={viewport}
          style={{ height, overflowY: "auto" }}
          onScroll={(e) => setScrollTop(e.currentTarget.scrollTop)}
        >
          <ul
            aria-label="Results"
            style={{
              height: result.items.length * rowHeight,
              position: "relative",
              margin: 0,
              padding: 0,
              listStyle: "none",
            }}
          >
            {result.items.slice(start, end).map((item, offset) => {
              const index = start + offset;
              return (
                <li
                  key={item.id}
                  aria-setsize={result.total}
                  aria-posinset={page * pageSize + index + 1}
                  style={{
                    position: "absolute",
                    top: index * rowHeight,
                    height: rowHeight,
                    left: 0,
                    right: 0,
                  }}
                >
                  {item.name}
                </li>
              );
            })}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}
    </section>
  );
}
```

The list reserves the full page height but mounts roughly ten visible rows plus overscan. Scrolling changes the slice; absolute row positions preserve the scrollbar. Pagination bounds the downloaded working set to one thousand rows even if total is above 100,000. Search is debounced by 250 ms, prior requests are aborted and a generation number rejects late responses even if cancellation is ignored. Effects clean up timers/requests when dependencies change or the component unmounts.

### API efficiency and changing data

Memoize `fetchPage` in the consuming app so its changing function identity does not refetch forever. Add a bounded cache keyed by normalized query, global sort, direction, page and data version; use ETags or short freshness policies where appropriate. A mutation increments `dataVersion` or invalidates affected cached queries and refetches the visible page. When filtering/deletion makes the page index invalid, clamp it using the returned total and request the nearest valid page.

Offset pages are easy for this sketch but can skip/duplicate entries during concurrent inserts. For a live mutable dataset, prefer cursor pagination over a stable `(sort_value,id)` order and, when necessary, a snapshot/version token. If the backend cannot perform global search/sort, load the full data intentionally and move that work into a Web Worker; copying 100,000 objects and stale worker results still need versioning and measurement.

### Test the user-visible behavior

Test the API contract with known records where a correct globally first item begins on another page. Use fake timers to verify the debounce and controlled promises to verify old responses never replace newer results. For 100,000 mocked records, assert the DOM row count remains near viewport plus overscan and check scroll positions at the beginning/middle/end. Exercise rapid search/sort/page changes, unmount, empty/error results and repeated mutations. Profile frame time and request count in a browser; a successful JSX compilation is not evidence of smooth scrolling.

Fixed-height virtualization requires truly fixed rows; wrapping text needs measurement or a variable-height virtualizer. Preserve list positions for assistive technology, and provide paginated/nonvirtual alternatives when users need reliable full-list navigation. This sample demonstrates the implementation; browser performance remains a measurement on the target application/device.

### Public contract and component boundaries

Assume ten to twenty displayed suggestions, typing around five keys/s and API latency 100–500 ms. The client accepts a provider function instead of hardcoding a URL:

```typescript
type Option = {id: string; label: string};
type Props = {
  value: string;
  onValueChange: (value: string) => void;
  loadOptions: (request: {query: string; limit: number; cursor?: string;
                         signal: AbortSignal}) => Promise<{
                           items: Option[]; nextCursor?: string}>;
  onSelect: (option: Option) => void;
  renderOption?: (option: Option, active: boolean) => React.ReactNode;
  label: string; disabled?: boolean;
};
```

Use `loadOptions({query,limit:20})` for the first page and the response cursor for subsequent pages under the **same** query/filter context. Deduplicate IDs when appending. Export `Autocomplete`, its public types and, if the library deliberately supports composition, documented styling primitives. Keep request generation IDs, cache machinery and highlight/portal internals private so consumers do not depend on unstable implementation details.

![W511. Frontend System Design: Autocomplete Search Input — architecture](../diagrams/W511_review_architecture.svg)

[Open the architecture diagram](../diagrams/W511_review_architecture.svg).

### Requests and cache correctness

Choose a roughly 200 ms debounce to avoid sending every keystroke, while showing loading state and keeping typing immediate. Throttling emits periodically during sustained input; it can suit continuous updates but sends more intermediate queries. Abort the previous request and also compare a monotonic request generation before committing results. Cancellation alone is insufficient if a provider ignores it. Reset active option/pagination when query context changes.

An LRU cache reuses **exact query plus locale/filter/user scope** results within a TTL, including cursor pages. A longer-prefix query may be filtered from a shorter-prefix cache only if the cached set is complete and the backend's matching/ranking semantics permit it; a top-twenty prefix result cannot represent every match for a longer query. Preloaded popular options help empty input and common requests but do not prove freshness or personalized relevance. Invalidate on account/locale/filter/provider-version changes and sensitive data boundaries.

### Accessibility, overlays and input behavior

Use a labeled input with `role="combobox"`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, `aria-autocomplete="list"` and `aria-activedescendant` pointing to a mounted option in a `role="listbox"`. Arrow keys update the active option and scroll it into view; Enter selects, Escape closes, and focus generally stays in the input. Mouse selection must not be lost when input blur runs first. Handle IME composition so Enter does not prematurely select while text is being composed.

Render the popup in a portal to escape clipping, position it relative to the input and update on scroll/resize. The portal manager tracks overlay ancestry, z-order and dismissal ownership, including the correct modal container/focus scope. A portal outside a modal must not accidentally become inert or hidden from assistive technology. With two open inputs, Escape goes to the topmost applicable active overlay and outside clicks dismiss according to that stack, not every dropdown on the page.

### Tests and large lists

Use fake timers for debounce, controlled promise resolution for stale-response races, keyboard tests for active ID/selection/Escape, pointer/blur tests and accessibility checks inside an overflow-hidden modal. Test both autocomplete instances together and cache separation between users. Virtualize only when a long list actually needs it: retain the active option in the mounted range, preserve correct option IDs/positions and test screen-reader navigation. Twenty suggestions usually do not justify that complexity.

### Give the board one owner of its rules

The `Minesweeper` object owns board dimensions, mine locations, revealed cells, flags, status and version. A cell's neighboring-mine count is derived from the immutable mine layout. A reveal of an empty cell performs breadth-first flood fill, expanding only through zero-count cells while also revealing their numbered boundary. Flags do not establish a win; revealing all non-mine cells does.

![W517. Minesweeper Game Design (OOD) — architecture](../diagrams/W517_review_architecture.svg)

[Open the architecture diagram](../diagrams/W517_review_architecture.svg).

### Runnable model

[Download the implementation and checks](../minesweeper_517.py).

```python
"""W517 local model; API state never exposes unrevealed mines."""

from collections import deque
import random


class Minesweeper:
    def __init__(self, rows, cols, mines, seed=0):
        if rows < 1 or cols < 1 or not 0 <= mines < rows * cols:
            raise ValueError("Need a nonempty board with at least one safe cell")
        self.rows = rows
        self.cols = cols
        self.mines = set(random.Random(seed).sample(range(rows * cols), mines))
        self.revealed = set()
        self.flagged = set()
        self.status = "playing"
        self.version = 0

    @classmethod
    def newGame(cls, rows, cols, mines, seed=0):
        return cls(rows, cols, mines, seed)

    def _index(self, r, c):
        if not 0 <= r < self.rows or not 0 <= c < self.cols:
            raise ValueError("Out of bounds")
        return r * self.cols + c

    def _neighbors(self, index):
        r, c = divmod(index, self.cols)
        for dr in (-1, 0, 1):
            for dc in (-1, 0, 1):
                if (dr or dc) and 0 <= r + dr < self.rows and 0 <= c + dc < self.cols:
                    yield (r + dr) * self.cols + c + dc

    def _count(self, index):
        return sum(n in self.mines for n in self._neighbors(index))

    def flag(self, r, c):
        index = self._index(r, c)
        if self.status != "playing" or index in self.revealed:
            return self.getState()
        if index in self.flagged:
            self.flagged.remove(index)
        else:
            self.flagged.add(index)
        self.version += 1
        return self.getState()

    def reveal(self, r, c):
        index = self._index(r, c)
        if self.status != "playing" or index in self.flagged or index in self.revealed:
            return self.getState()
        if index in self.mines:
            self.revealed.add(index)
            self.status = "lost"
        else:
            queue = deque([index])
            while queue:
                current = queue.popleft()
                if (
                    current in self.revealed
                    or current in self.flagged
                    or current in self.mines
                ):
                    continue
                self.revealed.add(current)
                if self._count(current) == 0:
                    queue.extend(self._neighbors(current))
            if len(self.revealed) == self.rows * self.cols - len(self.mines):
                self.status = "won"
        self.version += 1
        return self.getState()

    def getState(self):
        board = []
        for r in range(self.rows):
            row = []
            for c in range(self.cols):
                index = r * self.cols + c
                row.append(
                    "*"
                    if index in self.revealed and index in self.mines
                    else (
                        self._count(index)
                        if index in self.revealed
                        else "F" if index in self.flagged else "."
                    )
                )
            board.append(row)
        return dict(board=board, status=self.status, version=self.version)

    def snapshot(self):
        # Private persistence representation; never return this to a player.
        return dict(
            rows=self.rows,
            cols=self.cols,
            mines=sorted(self.mines),
            revealed=sorted(self.revealed),
            flagged=sorted(self.flagged),
            status=self.status,
            version=self.version,
        )

    @classmethod
    def restore(cls, snapshot):
        game = cls(snapshot["rows"], snapshot["cols"], 0)
        game.mines = set(snapshot["mines"])
        game.revealed = set(snapshot["revealed"])
        game.flagged = set(snapshot["flagged"])
        game.status = snapshot["status"]
        game.version = snapshot["version"]
        return game


if __name__ == "__main__":
    empty = Minesweeper(3, 3, 0)
    assert empty.reveal(1, 1)["status"] == "won"
    game = Minesweeper(2, 2, 1)
    mine = next(iter(game.mines))
    r, c = divmod(mine, 2)
    game.flag(r, c)
    before = game.version
    game.reveal(r, c)
    assert game.version == before
    game.flag(r, c)
    assert game.reveal(r, c)["status"] == "lost"
    assert Minesweeper.restore(game.snapshot()).getState() == game.getState()
    safe = Minesweeper(3, 3, 1)
    for cell in set(range(9)) - safe.mines:
        safe.reveal(*divmod(cell, 3))
    assert safe.status == "won"
    assert "*" not in str(Minesweeper(2, 2, 1).getState())
    print("W517 flood-fill, flags, loss, win, snapshot and hidden-state checks passed")
```

`newGame` returns a game instance, and the remaining methods act on it. This API chooses toggle semantics for `flag`. Revealing flagged/already revealed cells and commands after completion are no-ops. It allows a mine on the first click; if a guaranteed-safe first click is required, place mines after that click while excluding the chosen cell (and optionally its neighbors). That changes generation, not flood-fill rules.

Worst-case reveal work is linear in board size. Each cell has at most eight neighbors; revisits are skipped. A queue avoids recursive stack overflow on large empty boards. The seed is for repeatable tests, not a public competitive-board secret.

### Persistence, scores and multi-device synchronization

Store the **private** snapshot, owner, rules/version, server start/finish times and an append-only command ID log. The public `getState` deliberately excludes hidden mines. Do not let an HTTP serializer accidentally expose `snapshot`; mine locations and seeds defeat the puzzle.

For multi-device use, submit `{game_id,command_id,expected_version,action}`. Serialize commands per game using an owner or transactional compare-and-swap. Deduplicate command IDs, especially because retrying a flag toggle twice would undo it. Persist state/version before acknowledging; broadcast the new safe view or delta. A stale device receives the latest version and must reapply an intentional action rather than overwriting the whole board.

Leaderboard entries come from server-validated completed games and trusted elapsed time, keyed by unique game ID so replay cannot add another score. Partition rankings by board/rules/difficulty; different boards are not automatically comparable. Apply rate limits and integrity checks before accepting competitive scores. The local tests cover zero-mine flood fill, flag protection/toggle, loss, safe-cell win and snapshot restoration; concurrent API persistence requires integration tests in the chosen backend.

question:
Implement a TradeProcessor class. It accepts incoming trades, lets clients subscribe to trades matching a filter with a callback, returns a handle for the subscription, and supports unsubscription. This is a coding-flavored design round where the prompt is deliberately under-specified — clarification is half the work.

Reported interface:

using HANDLE = int;
struct Trade { string symbol; int size; string flag; };
class TradeProcessor {
void onNewTrade(const Trade& trade);
HANDLE subscribe(const Trade& tradeFilter, function<void(const Trade&)> callback);
void unsubscribe(HANDLE handle);
};

Clarify before coding: what does the filter match on (exact symbol? flag? prefix?), is onNewTrade single- or multi-threaded, are callbacks invoked synchronously inline or dispatched, and does unsubscribe guarantee no further callbacks fire after it returns.

solution.teaching_notes_markdown:
### Resolve the ambiguous contract

Choose the following explicit contract: one calling thread, synchronous callbacks, exact matching for nonempty symbol/flag and positive size, with empty strings and size zero as wildcards. Conditions combine with AND. `unsubscribe` is idempotent and prevents any **later** invocation in this single-thread model; it cannot undo a callback already executing. These are chosen semantics because the prompt leaves them open.

![W539. Trade Processor with Subscribe / Unsubscribe — architecture](../diagrams/W539_review_architecture.svg)

[Open the architecture diagram](../diagrams/W539_review_architecture.svg).

### Complete C++ implementation

[Download the C++ source and assertions](../trade_processor_539.cpp). Compile with `clang++ -std=c++17 trade_processor_539.cpp -o trades` and run `./trades`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cassert>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
#include <map>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using HANDLE = int;
struct Trade { std::string symbol; int size; std::string flag; };

// Contract: one calling thread; callbacks execute synchronously and may subscribe/unsubscribe.
// Empty symbol/flag and size==0 are wildcards; other fields match exactly, combined with AND.
class TradeProcessor {
    struct Subscription { Trade filter; std::function<void(const Trade&)> callback; };
    std::map<HANDLE,Subscription> subscriptions;
    HANDLE next_handle = 1;
    static bool matches(const Trade& t, const Trade& f) {
        return (f.symbol.empty() || t.symbol==f.symbol)
            && (f.size==0 || t.size==f.size)
            && (f.flag.empty() || t.flag==f.flag);
    }
public:
    HANDLE subscribe(const Trade& filter, std::function<void(const Trade&)> callback) {
        if (!callback || filter.size<0) throw std::invalid_argument("Invalid subscription");
        if(next_handle==std::numeric_limits<HANDLE>::max())
            throw std::overflow_error("Handle space exhausted");
        HANDLE handle=next_handle++;
        subscriptions.emplace(handle,Subscription{filter,std::move(callback)});
        return handle;
    }
    void unsubscribe(HANDLE handle) { subscriptions.erase(handle); }
    void onNewTrade(const Trade& trade) {
        std::vector<HANDLE> handles;
        for(const auto& pair: subscriptions) handles.push_back(pair.first);
        for(HANDLE handle: handles) {
            auto found=subscriptions.find(handle);
            if(found==subscriptions.end() || !matches(trade,found->second.filter)) continue;
            // Copy before calling: callback may erase its own map entry.
            auto callback=found->second.callback;
            try { callback(trade); }
            catch(const std::exception& error) { std::cerr<<"Callback failed: "<<error.what()<<'\n'; }
            catch(...) { std::cerr<<"Callback failed with nonstandard exception\n"; }
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    TradeProcessor processor;
    int exact=0, any=0;
    auto first=processor.subscribe({"AAPL",100,"X"},[&](const Trade&){++exact;});
    processor.subscribe({"",0,""},[&](const Trade&){++any;});
    processor.onNewTrade({"AAPL",100,"X"});
    processor.onNewTrade({"AAPL",101,"X"});
    assert(exact==1 && any==2);
    processor.unsubscribe(first); processor.unsubscribe(first);
    processor.onNewTrade({"AAPL",100,"X"}); assert(exact==1 && any==3);
    TradeProcessor reentrant; HANDLE later=0;
    reentrant.subscribe({"",0,""},[&](const Trade&){reentrant.unsubscribe(later);});
    later=reentrant.subscribe({"",0,""},[&](const Trade&){assert(false);});
    reentrant.onNewTrade({"X",1,""});
    TradeProcessor self; HANDLE own=0; int count=0;
    own=self.subscribe({"",0,""},[&](const Trade&){++count;self.unsubscribe(own);});
    self.onNewTrade({"X",1,""}); self.onNewTrade({"X",1,""});assert(count==1);
    std::cout<<"W539 filter, unsubscribe and reentrancy checks passed\n";
}

```

The processor snapshots handles, not callbacks. Immediately before each invocation it checks whether that handle still exists. Thus callback A can unsubscribe B and B will not run later in the same dispatch. New subscriptions do not receive the trade already being dispatched. Copying a callback before invoking it keeps self-unsubscription from destroying the callable currently in use. Handles are never reused within the checked integer range, avoiding stale handles accidentally removing new subscriptions.

Callback exceptions are isolated and reported so one subscriber does not stop all others. For a production logger, avoid synchronous slow output on a latency-sensitive path. Each trade scans S subscriptions, so delivery is $O(S)$ plus callback work; subscribe/unsubscribe use the ordered map's $O(\log S)$ operations. At larger scale, index by symbol and wildcard bucket, then check remaining fields.

### Explain the multithreading follow-up

This implementation is deliberately **not** thread-safe. Adding one mutex around everything and calling user callbacks while holding it risks deadlocks and slow-subscriber stalls. A concurrent implementation needs a registry lock plus per-subscription active/in-flight state, invokes callbacks outside the registry lock, and defines cancellation semantics.

If unsubscribe must wait until no callback can run after it returns, it must deactivate the subscription and wait for in-flight work to drain. Self-unsubscribe cannot wait for its own callback to finish; define a special nonblocking self-cancel rule or disallow that strong operation from callbacks. Asynchronous delivery additionally needs bounded queues, overflow policy, per-subscriber ordering and cancellation of queued work. State those guarantees before changing the code.

